# A realm used WITHOUT MONEY is not a virgin realm either.
#
# testclock.gno's arming gate has three parts, and this file exists for the one
# that no other harness can reach:
#
#     if CourtCount() > 1        { ... already has courts ... }
#     if m.nextID > 1 || m.coin.TotalSupply() > 0 { ... has been used ... }
#
# The court count alone was the AUDITED BUG, as EnableTestClock's own comment on
# the meta court records: a realm with months of activity on the meta court, and
# no second court, called itself virgin and armed. The fix added the second line,
# whose two arms are two different ways to have a history: claims, and coin.
#
# WHY A TXTAR, AND WHY TWO FILES. The arming gate is unreachable from both
# cheaper harnesses. The in-package suite short-circuits on the court count
# (some earlier test has always started a court by then), and a filetest's
# caller is never kourtv2's deployer, so it stops at mustDeployer — which is all
# z_testclock_filetest.gno can assert. Only here is the deployer the one calling.
#
# Each arm needs its own file because the state only accumulates. Isolating the
# CLAIMS arm needs nextID > 1 while the supply is still zero, and isolating the
# COIN arm needs supply > 0 while nextID is still <= 1 — and once coin is minted
# it never returns to zero. This file takes the first; kourtv2_usedrealm_coin.txtar
# takes the second. Measured, not assumed: a mutation deleting either arm on its
# own SURVIVES the whole mutation gate, because that gate runs the unit suite,
# which never reaches the line. (No row count here on purpose: the first version
# of this line quoted one, and the commit that wrote it moved the corpus past it.)
#
# The lever is OpenClaimSeeded, which waives the CC deposit and the fee for a
# moderator bootstrapping a young court (claim.gno, OpenClaimSeeded). It is the
# reason the claims arm is not redundant with the coin one: seeding is how a court
# acquires a history without anybody ever buying a coin.

loadpkg gno.land/p/kourt/checkpoint/v0
loadpkg gno.land/p/kourt/grc20votes/v0
loadpkg gno.land/p/kourt/governor/v0
loadpkg gno.land/p/kourt/twap/v0
loadpkg gno.land/p/kourt/curve/v0
loadpkg gno.land/r/kourt/kourtv2

gnoland start

# --- On arrival: one court, no claims, no coin ---------------------------------
# The three readings the gate reads, asserted before anything touches them, so a
# later divergence is attributable. This is also the state in which arming is
# SUPPOSED to succeed — kourtv2_testclock.txtar arms from exactly here.
gnokey query vm/qeval --data "gno.land/r/kourt/kourtv2.CourtCount()"
stdout '\(1 int\)'
gnokey query vm/qeval --data "gno.land/r/kourt/kourtv2.ClaimCount(\"meta\")"
stdout '\(0 uint64\)'
gnokey query vm/qeval --data "gno.land/r/kourt/kourtv2.CoinSupply(\"meta\")"
stdout '\(0 int64\)'

# --- Give the realm a history, with no coin anywhere in it ---------------------
# Two claims, because the arm is `nextID > 1`: the first seeded claim leaves
# nextID at 1 and does NOT make the realm used by this arm. That tolerance is
# deliberate and is NOT asserted here — asserting it would mean arming
# successfully at one claim, which consumes the one-shot latch and ends the file.
# It is harmless: OpenClaimSeeded needs an active moderator, and meta's only
# moderator at deploy is the deployer, who may arm a pristine realm anyway. A
# stranger's only route to using this realm is Buy, which the coin arm catches.
gnokey maketx call -pkgpath gno.land/r/kourt/kourtv2 -func OpenClaimSeeded -args meta -args 'Seeded while the realm was still young' -gas-fee 1000000ugnot -gas-wanted 200000000 -broadcast -chainid=tendermint_test test1
stdout 'OK!'
gnokey maketx call -pkgpath gno.land/r/kourt/kourtv2 -func OpenClaimSeeded -args meta -args 'And a second, so nextID passes 1' -gas-fee 1000000ugnot -gas-wanted 200000000 -broadcast -chainid=tendermint_test test1
stdout 'OK!'

# The whole point of this file: real history, and the coin supply STILL ZERO.
# Without this assertion the refusal below would not distinguish the two arms.
gnokey query vm/qeval --data "gno.land/r/kourt/kourtv2.ClaimCount(\"meta\")"
stdout '\(2 uint64\)'
gnokey query vm/qeval --data "gno.land/r/kourt/kourtv2.CoinSupply(\"meta\")"
stdout '\(0 int64\)'

# And still only the one court, so the count arm is not what refuses below.
gnokey query vm/qeval --data "gno.land/r/kourt/kourtv2.CourtCount()"
stdout '\(1 int\)'

# --- The deployer is now refused ----------------------------------------------
# test1 IS the deployer here (it addpkg'd kourtv2), so mustDeployer passes and
# the refusal can only come from the usage gate. Delete `m.nextID > 1` from
# testclock.gno and this line is what fails.
! gnokey maketx call -pkgpath gno.land/r/kourt/kourtv2 -func EnableTestClock -gas-fee 1000000ugnot -gas-wanted 200000000 -broadcast -chainid=tendermint_test test1
stderr 'this realm has been used'

# EnableTestClockAt takes the same path (it calls EnableTestClock first), so the
# scenario-friendly entrypoint is not a way around the gate.
! gnokey maketx call -pkgpath gno.land/r/kourt/kourtv2 -func EnableTestClockAt -args 1750000000 -gas-fee 1000000ugnot -gas-wanted 200000000 -broadcast -chainid=tendermint_test test1
stderr 'this realm has been used'

# --- The refusal is a refusal: the latch is still shut afterwards --------------
# What this does NOT catch, stated because the first version of this comment
# claimed it did: a half-written latch. tcEverArmed is written on the line after
# tcArmed, but an aborted MsgCall is discarded whole, so no partial write is ever
# observable by a later query — injecting `tcArmed = true` immediately before the
# usage-gate panic leaves both these files passing. Transaction atomicity does
# that job and a txtar cannot test it.
#
# What it does pin is the post-condition an operator actually reads: the chain
# still says its dates are its own. TestClockFabricated is the disclosure every
# reader of a date here is entitled to, and it is written by the arming path, so
# an arming that half-succeeded in some FUTURE refactor — one that committed —
# would show true. TestClockSkew is deliberately not read: no arming path writes
# it (only AdvanceTestClock does), so asserting it here would measure nothing.
gnokey query vm/qeval --data "gno.land/r/kourt/kourtv2.TestClockActive()"
stdout 'false'
gnokey query vm/qeval --data "gno.land/r/kourt/kourtv2.TestClockFabricated()"
stdout 'false'

# --- AND THE COURT-COUNT ARM, which its own audit named and nothing exercised ---
# `CourtCount() > 1` was the AUDITED BUG this file's header names, and up to here the
# count has been 1, so every refusal above came from the usage gate. One user court
# takes the count to 2 and the count arm becomes the FIRST thing that refuses — a
# different message, which is what makes this an assertion about that arm and not a
# second copy of the one above. Nothing anywhere reached it: widening the check to
# `> 2` survives the gno suite (which cannot arm at all) and survived both of these
# files (which never held two courts).
gnokey maketx call -pkgpath gno.land/r/kourt/kourtv2 -func StartCourt -args orem -args 'Orem Truth Court' -gas-fee 1000000ugnot -gas-wanted 200000000 -broadcast -chainid=tendermint_test test1
stdout 'OK!'
gnokey query vm/qeval --data "gno.land/r/kourt/kourtv2.CourtCount()"
stdout '\(2 int\)'

! gnokey maketx call -pkgpath gno.land/r/kourt/kourtv2 -func EnableTestClock -gas-fee 1000000ugnot -gas-wanted 200000000 -broadcast -chainid=tendermint_test test1
stderr 'this realm already has courts'
